    "pressure": {
      "file": "labyrinth_seal_pressure.glb",
      "title": "Pressure drops step by step",
      "description": "A labyrinth seal is a non-contacting seal: gas leaking along the shaft is forced through a series of tiny clearances at the knife-edge teeth. At each tooth it accelerates and loses pressure; in the cavity after it the jet's kinetic energy is dissipated as turbulence instead of being recovered. Pressure therefore falls in steps from the high-pressure side to the low-pressure side. Leakage falls with more teeth and smaller clearance, but never reaches zero; a buffer-gas or eductor port at a middle cavity can stop process gas escaping.",
      "render": "normal",
      "annotations": []
    }
  },
